\begin{theorem}\label{thm:Characterization}
A mechanism $\{w_\th,\mu_\th\}_{\th\in\Th}$ is incentive compatible if and only if there exists a generalized recommendation schedule $\{\mu_{\th,\hat\th}\}_{(\th,\hat\th)\in\Th^2}$ with $\mu_{\th,\th}=\mu_\th$ and an absolutely continuous function $V$ such that $V(\th) = U(\th, w_\th, \mu_\th)$ and the following conditions are jointly satisfied for all $\th\in\Th$:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Envelope:} 
         \vspace*{-.2em}
    $$V(\theta) = V(\underline{\theta})-\int_{\underline{\theta}}^{\theta} \frac{\partial}{\partial \th} C(t,\mu_{t})\de t;$$

    \item \textbf{Generalized Monotonicity:} 
         \vspace*{-.2em}
    $$\int_{\hat{\theta}}^{\theta}\Big[- \frac{\partial C(t,\mu_{t})}{\partial \th }+ \frac{\partial C(t,\mu_{t,\hat{\theta}})}{\partial \th }\Big]\de t \geq 0 \quad \text{for all } \ \hat{\theta};$$

    \item \textbf{On-path Obedience:}%\footnote{A complete characterization also requires the payments associated to off the support outputs to be sufficiently small. We can always choose the wages off-path to be equal to the wage for the closest smaller output that is on path.}
         \vspace*{-.2em}
$$w_{\theta}(x) \begin{cases}
       \le  u^{-1}\Big(c_{\mu_{\theta}}(\th, x)+V(\theta)+C(\th,\mu_{\theta})-\int_X c_{\mu_{\theta}}(y)\mu_{\theta}(\th,\de y)\Big)
    \quad \text{for all } x \in X,
    \\
 =  u^{-1}\Big(c_{\mu_{\theta}}(\th, x)+V(\theta)+C(\th,\mu_{\theta})-\int_X c_{\mu_{\theta}}(y)\mu_{\theta}(\th,\de y)\Big)  \quad \mu_{\theta}\text{-almost all } x\in X;
\end{cases}
$$
     \item \textbf{Off-path Obedience:}
     \vspace*{-.2em}
\begin{equation*}
    \mu_{\th, \hat{\th}} \in \underset{\nu\in \D(X)}{\argmax} \int_X \Big[u\circ w_{\hat{\theta}}(x)-c_{\mu_{\th, \hat{\th}}}(\th,x)\Big]\nu(\de x) \qquad \text{ for all } \hat\th \ne \th.
\end{equation*}

\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}


The difficulty in characterizing feasible mechanisms in our problem is that the agent may double deviate. %: he can misreport his type and then implement a different distribution than recommended for his report.  
A tractable characterization of all incentive compatible mechanisms in problems with both adverse selection and moral hazard has so far been elusive because of the vast class of potential deviations \citep[see][for a discussion of this difficulty]{castro2024disentangling}. 
Introducing the  generalized recommendation schedule enables us to explicitly keep track of the relevant continuation strategy for any type after any misreport. We combine this approach with a flexible moral hazard problem, which gives  necessary and sufficient first order conditions for wages to implement any action. This is particularly useful here because the wages that implement the on-path recommendation  
 schedule $\{\mu_\th\}_{\th\in\Th}$ directly pin down the off-path recommendation schedule $\{\mu_{\th,\hat\th}\}_{(\th,\hat\th)\in\Th^2}$. 

 The envelope and first-order conditions in items 1 and 3 are  the standard conditions in the isolated adverse selection problem \citep[item 1, see e.g.][]{milgrom2002envelope} and flexible moral hazard problem \citepalias[item 3, see][]{georgiadis2024flexible}.

 Items 2 and 4 deal with the interplay between adverse selection and moral hazard. Item 2 mirrors the monotonicity constraint in classical adverse selection problems. To see the parallels, suppose the agent's action $\mu$ was contractible so that deviations from the recommended choice can be deterred. Then $\mu_{\th, \hat \th} = \mu_{\hat \th}$ for any true type $\th$. Suppose further for simplicity that $C(\th, \mu) = (1-\th) \k(\mu) $ for some cost function $\k $. In that case, the monotonicity constraint becomes $\int_{\hat\th}^\th \Brac{\k (\mu_{t}) -\k (\mu_{\hat \th})}\de t \ge 0$, which says that more productive types must be asked to implement costlier distributions. In general, however, the monotonicity condition in item 2 must take into account the potential for double deviations. %We will see examples below in which this condition becomes ``if the agent reports a higher type than his true type, he must choose a costlier distribution after the  

Finally, item 4 ensures that $\mu_{\th,\hat\th}$ is indeed an optimal continuation strategy after type $\th$ reported $\hat\th$. 
Note that imposing the obedience condition in item 4 for $\hat \theta = \th$ would imply the condition on wages  in item 3, with one additional degree of freedom: to satisfy obedience alone, the term $V(\theta)+C(\th,\mu_{\theta})-\int_X c_{\mu_{\theta}}(y)\mu_{\theta}(\th,\de y)$ could be replaced by any constant (independent of $x$). The truthtelling constraint from the hidden-type component determines this constant. 

\section{Applications}\label{sec:applications}
We illustrate \Cref{thm:Characterization} with several examples. Note that we have not specified the principal's preferences so far as the characterization above is independent of them. In the examples below, unless otherwise stated, assume that the principal is risk neutral and his final payoff after output realization $x$ and paying wage $w$ is $x-w$. Assume further that $X=[\ul x, \bar x]$, as well as $0<\ul\th<\bar\th<1$ and that $F\in \D(\Th)$ admits an everywhere positive density $f$. 

\paragraph{Example 1.} \label{Example1} Let $u(w) = w$,  and $C(\th, \mu) = (1-\th) \int_X z(x)\mu(\de x)$, where $z(\cdot)$ is differentiable, strictly increasing and convex. 

Under this specification, the Gâteaux derivative is $c_{\mu}(\th, x) = (1-\th)z(x)$ for any distribution $\mu$. 
By the definition of $V$ and item 1 in \Cref{thm:Characterization}, any incentive compatible mechanism satisfies 
$$ 
V(\theta) 
= \int_X w_{\theta}(x)\mu_{\theta}(\de x)-(1-\theta) \int_X z(x)\mu_\th(\de x) 
= V(\underline{\theta})+\int_{\underline{\theta}}^{\theta}\Paren{\int_X z(y)\mu_{t}(\de y)}\de t.$$
Solving for the expected wage payment gives
$$ 
\int_X w_{\theta}(x)\mu_{\theta}(\de x) = (1-\theta)\int_X z(x)\mu_\th(\de x)+V(\underline{\theta})  +\int_{\underline{\theta}}^{\theta} \Paren{\int_X z(y)\mu_{t}(\de y)}\de t.$$
Inserting the wage into the principal's expected payoff and  integrating  by parts gives
\begin{equation}\label{Eqriskneutral}
\begin{split}
    \int_{\underline{\theta}}^{\overline{\theta}}\int_X [x-w_{\theta}(x)]\mu_{\theta}(\de x)\de F(\theta) 
    %&= \int_{\underline{\theta}}^{\overline{\theta}}\int \Big\{x-\Big[1-\theta+\frac{1-F(\theta)}{f(\theta)}\Big]C(\mu_{\theta})\Big\}\mu_{\theta}(\de x)\de F(\theta)-V(\underline{\theta}) \\
    & = \int_{\underline{\theta}}^{\overline{\theta}}\int_X \Big\{x-\Big[1-\theta+\frac{1-F(\theta)}{f(\theta)}\Big]z(x)\Big\}\mu_{\theta}(\de x)\de F(\theta)-V(\underline{\theta}).
    \end{split}
\end{equation}
With the characterization in \Cref{thm:Characterization}, we thus write the Principal's revenue maximization problem as\footnote{Recall that $\mu_\th = \mu_{\th,\th}$ by the definition of the generalized recommendation schedule. }
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Ex1Objective}
    \underset{\{w_{\th}\}_\th, \{\mu_{\th,\hat\th}\}_{(\th,\hat\th)}, V(\underline{\theta})}{max} \; \int_{\underline{\theta}}^{\overline{\theta}}\int_X \Big\{x-\Big[1-\theta+\frac{1-F(\theta)}{f(\theta)}\Big]z(x)\Big\}\mu_{\theta}(\de x)\de F(\theta)-V(\underline{\theta})
\end{equation}
subject to
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Ex1Mon}\tag{Monotonicity}
    \int_{\hat{\theta}}^{\theta}\int_X z(x)\big[\mu_t - \mu_{t,\hat\th}\big] (\de x)\de t \geq 0 \qquad \text{ for all } \ \theta,\hat{\theta}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\tag{Obedience}
    \mu_{\theta,\hat{\theta}} \in \underset{\mu\in \D(X) }{arg \ max} \int_X \Big[u\circ w_{\hat{\theta}}(x)-(1-\th)z(x) \Big]\mu(\de x) \quad \text{ for all } \ \theta,\hat{\theta} 
\end{equation}
The Obedience constraint (reflecting items 3 and 4 in \Cref{thm:Characterization}) shows the Gâteaux derivative $c_{\mu_{\theta,\hat \theta}} (\th, x) = (1-\th)z(x)$.

In this example we can maximize the objective \eqref{eq:Ex1Objective} pointwise for each $\th$, disregarding both the monotonicity and obedience constraints and then check when they are satisfied by the solution. 
First, observe that optimally $V(\underline{\theta})=0$.
As $z$ is strictly convex, the objective in \eqref{eq:Ex1Objective} at point $\th$ has a unique maximizer $$
x_\th = \underset{x\in X}{\argmax} \Big\{ x - \Big[1-\th +\frac{1-F(\th)}{f(\th)}\Big] z(x)\Big\}.
$$
Therefore, it is pointwise optimal to set $\mu_{\theta} = \delta _{\{x_{\theta}\}}$, where $\delta_{\{x\}}$ denotes the Dirac measure on $x$. 


An optimal wage function to satisfy obedience is
$$w^*_\th(x) 
= \begin{cases}
    \ul w & \text{if } x < x_\th,
    \\
     (1-\th) z(x_\th) + \int_{\ul \th}^\th  z(x_t) \de t & \text{if } x \ge  x_\th, 
\end{cases}
$$
with a sufficiently low constant $\ul w$ (see item 3 in \Cref{thm:Characterization}). 

When the on-path recommendation consists of a Dirac measure, the principal can perfectly observe whether the agent follows this recommendation. Hence, off-path Obedience follows from on-path Obedience with $\mu_{\th, \hat \th} = \mu_{\hat \th}$ for all $\th$. 

Finally, we need to verify whether the monotonicity constraint is satisfied. 
With $\mu_{t,\hat\th} = \mu_{\hat \th}$, \eqref{eq:Ex1Mon}  above is equivalent to $x_\th$ being non-decreasing in $\th$, the usual monotonicity constraint in standard screening problems.  
Note that if the maximizer $x_\th$ is interior, it satisfies 
$$z^{\prime}(x_{\theta}) = \Big[1-\theta + \frac{1-F(\theta)}{f(\theta)}\Big]^{-1}.$$
 As $z$ is convex, $x_{\theta}$ increases iff $1-\theta + \frac{1-F(\theta)}{f(\theta)}$ decreases in $\theta$, the \textit{regular} monotone virtual value condition. \hfil \#


 % \begin{remark}
 %     Whenever the recommended on-path distribution ($\mu^{*}_{\theta}$) is a degenerate distribution, the monotonicity constraint becomes the usual monotonicity constraint for standard screening problems. This might be useful beyond this example.
 % \end{remark}
 %  \begin{remark}
 %     Two very restrictive assumptions were useful in this example: 1- Assuming that $u(w)=w$ allows us to use the integration by parts trick. 2-  Assuming that $C(\mu)= \int z(x)\mu(\de x)$ allows us to maximize pointwise on $x$.
 % \end{remark}

\paragraph{Example 1b.}
We maintain the assumptions from Example 1 with the exception of the cost function, which we assume to be $C(\th,\mu) = (1-\th) K\Paren{\int_X z(x) \mu(\de x) }$, for a strictly increasing and convex function $K\colon \real \to \real $. 

Applying analogous steps to Example 1, the principal's objective, pointwise for each $\th$, is equal to 
  \begin{equation}\label{Eq:Ex1Obj}
     \underset{\mu}{\max} \ \int_X \bigg[ x-\Big[1-\theta+\frac{1-F(\theta)}{f(\theta)}\Big]K\Big(\int_X z(y) \mu(\de y)\Big)\bigg]\mu(\de x).
  \end{equation}
  Given the monotonicity of $K$ and the convexity of $z$, the above objective increases whenever we replace a candidate distribution $\mu$ with a Dirac measure on the expected value. Thus,  
it is still optimal to choose degenerate distributions $\delta_{\{x_{ \th}\}}$. 
It follows analogously to Example 1 that, when interior,  $x_\th$ is characterized by:
  $$K'\Paren{z(x_\th)}z^{\prime}(x_{\theta}) = \Big[1-\theta + \frac{1-F(\theta)}{f(\theta)}\Big]^{-1}.$$
By convexity of $K$, monotonicity is again satisfied if $1-\theta + \frac{1-F(\theta)}{f(\theta)}$ is decreasing in $\th$.  \hfill \#


\paragraph{Example 2.} Let $u(w) = w$,  and $C(\th, \mu) = (1-\th) K\Paren{\int_X z(x)\mu(\de x)}$, where $K$ is strictly increasing and convex and $z(\cdot)$ is differentiable, strictly increasing and concave. Thus, the concavity of $z$ is the only difference with respect to Example 1b. 

Replicating the arguments from the previous example, we get to the same $\th$-pointwise maximization in  \eqref{Eq:Ex1Obj}. 

For this example, we first show that $supp\big(\mu_{\theta}\big)\subseteq \{\underline{x},\overline{x}\}$ for all $\th$.
Consider any candidate $\mu$ and replace it by the binary distribution $\tilde{\mu}$ satisfying  $\int y\tilde{\mu}(\de y) = \int y\mu(\de y)$ and $supp\big(\tilde{\mu}\big) \subseteq \{\underline{x},\overline{x}\}$. Clearly, $\tilde{\mu}$ is a mean-preserving spread of $\mu$. 
Since $z$ is strictly concave, $\int z(y) \tilde{\mu}(\de y) \leq \int z(y) \mu(\de y)$ with a strict inequality whenever $\mu\big( X \backslash  \{\underline{x},\overline{x}\}\big) > 0 $. Moreover, as $K$ is increasing and $\Big[1-\theta+\frac{1-F(\theta)}{f(\theta)}\Big]> 0$, we have that 
  \begin{equation*}
  \begin{split}
     \int_X x \mu(\de x)-  \Big[1-\theta+\frac{1-F(\theta)}{f(\theta)}\Big]K\Big(&\int_X z(y) \mu(\de y)\Big)%\int z(x)\mu(\de x)
     \\
     \leq & \int_X x \tilde{\mu}(\de x)-  \Big[1-\theta+\frac{1-F(\theta)}{f(\theta)}\Big]K\Big(\int_X z(y) \tilde{\mu}(\de y)\Big)
     %\int_X z(x)\tilde{\mu}(\de x),
     \end{split}
  \end{equation*}
  with strict inequality whenever $\mu\big( X \backslash  \{\underline{x},\overline{x}\}\big) > 0 $. Therefore, any pointwise maximizing distribution $\mu_\th$ must satisfy  $supp\big(\mu_{\theta}\big)\subseteq \{\underline{x},\overline{x}\}$. 
Since only $x\in\{\ul x,\bar x\}$ will be observed, below we use the shorthand 
$$\ul z := z(\ul x) \qquad \text{ and } \qquad  \bar z := z(\bar x).
$$ 
We can represent the binary distribution $\mu_{\theta} $ by a $p_{\theta}\in [0,1]$ which denotes the probability of $\bar{x}$. The pointwise maximization of the principal becomes 
\begin{equation}\label{EqEx4}
\underset{p\in[0,1]}{\max} \ \Big\{ \underline{x}+p(\overline{x}-\ul x) - \Big[1-\theta+\frac{1-F(\theta)}{f(\theta)}\Big]K\Big(\ul z +p (\bar z - \ul z) \Big)\Big\}.
\end{equation}

To determine an optimal wage $w_\th$ that satisfies on-path obedience, use \Cref{thm:Characterization}, item 3. With the specification in this example, the Gâteaux derivative given a distribution $\mu$  is $ c_\mu(\th,x) = (1-\th)K'\Paren{\int_X z(y) \de \mu(y) }z(x)$. Hence, with the binary distribution, we can set wages 
\begin{align*}
    w_\th(x) = 
    \begin{cases}
        (1-\th)\Brac{K\Paren{\ul z + p_\th (\bar z - \ul z) } -K'\Paren{\ul z + p_\th (\bar z - \ul z)  }p_\th \Paren{\bar z - \ul z  }}  + \int_{\ul\th}^\th K\Paren{\ul z + p_t(\bar z -\ul z) }\de t &\text{ if } x< \bar x
        \\
       (1-\th)\Brac{K\Paren{\ul z + p_\th (\bar z - \ul z)  } + K'\Paren{\ul z + p_\th (\bar z - \ul z)  }(1-p_\th) \Paren{\bar z - \ul z  }}  + \int_{\ul\th}^\th K\Paren{\ul z + p_t(\bar z -\ul z) }\de t &\text{ if } x=  \bar x.
    \end{cases}
\end{align*} 
It is easily verified that the wage difference $w_\th (\bar x) - w_\th (\ul x) $ equals the difference in Gâteaux derivatives  
$c_{\mu_\th}(\th, \bar x) - c_{\mu_\th}(\th, \ul x) = (1-\th) K'\Paren{\ul z +p_\th (\bar z -\ul z)}[\bar z - \ul z]$.

Now consider the off-path recommendations $\mu_{\th,\hat \th}$. Given wages $w_{\hat\th}$, putting probability mass on any $x \in (\ul x, \bar x)$ is strictly dominated by placing the corresponding mass on $\ul x$. Hence, $supp(\mu_{\th, \hat \th} )\subseteq \{\ul x, \bar x\}$ also for $\th \ne \hat \th$. 
For the binary distribution $\mu_{\th,\hat \th}$, denote by $p_{\th,\hat \th}$ the probability of output $\bar x$. 
By item 4 in \Cref{thm:Characterization}, $p_{\th,\hat \th}$ must solve\footnote{We write the first order condition in terms of the Gâteaux derivative for consistency with the Theorem statement. In this one-dimensional problem it is equally simple to consider the direct maximization of $w_{\hat{\theta}}(\underline{x})+p\big( w_{\hat{\theta}}(\overline{x})-w_{\hat{\theta}}(\underline{x})\big) - (1-\theta)K\big(\ul z + p(\bar z - \ul z)\big) $. } 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Ex2OffPath}
   \underset{p\in [0,1]}{\max} \Big\{ w_{\hat{\theta}}(\underline{x})+p\big( w_{\hat{\theta}}(\overline{x})-w_{\hat{\theta}}(\underline{x})\big) - (1-\theta)K'\big(\ul z + p_{\th,\hat \th}(\bar z - \ul z)\big) \Paren{\ul z + p(\bar z - \ul z)} \Big\}.
  \end{equation}


The generalized monotonicity constraint (item 2 in \Cref{thm:Characterization}) becomes 
\begin{align*}
    \int_{\hat \th}^\th \Brac{ K(\ul z + p_t(\bar z - \ul z)) - K(\ul z + p_{t,\hat\th}(\bar z - \ul z)) }\de t \ge 0 \qquad \text{ for all } \th, \hat \th ,  
\end{align*}
  which is satisfied if $p_{\th,\hat \th}$ is increasing in $\hat \th$. 
  By \eqref{eq:Ex2OffPath}, this is the case whenever the wage difference $ w_{\hat{\theta}}(\overline{x})-w_{\hat{\theta}}(\underline{x})$ increases in $\hat \th$. 
This is satisfied if the difference in Gâteaux derivatives is increasing in type: 
$$
c_{\mu_\th}(\th, \bar x) - c_{\mu_\th}(\th, \ul x) = (1-\th) K'\Paren{\ul z +p_\th (\bar z -\ul z)}[\bar z - \ul z].
$$



The first-order condition for the pointwise maximization of the principal's payoff in $p_\th$ is 
\begin{equation}\label{FOCp}
   \frac{ \overline{x}-\underline{x}}{\bar z -\ul z} -\Big[1-\theta+\frac{1-F(\theta)}{f(\theta)}\Big]K'\Big(\ul z + p_\th (\bar z - \ul z)\Big) \begin{cases} 
     \leq 0  \quad \text{if} \ p_{\theta} = 0,\\
     = 0 \quad \text{if} \ p_{\theta} \in (0,1), \\
     \geq 0 \quad \text{if} \ p_{\theta} = 1.
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
We see that the principal optimally asks the agent to produce maximal output with probability 1 if the increase in output from lowest to highest exceeds the increase in per-unit cost $z$ by more than a virtual cost factor. This cost factor is itself increasing in the probability of the high output through the convexity of $K$. 


Thus, for types with $p_\theta \in (0,1)$, de difference in wages is increasing if and only if 
    \begin{equation*}
    \frac{1-\th}{1-\th + \frac{1-F(\th)}{f(\th)}}[\bar x - \ul x]
  \end{equation*}
is increasing in $\th$.   
The expression above is increasing whenever $(1-\theta)f(\theta)/(1-F(\theta))$ is increasing in $\theta$. 
While this condition is more restrictive, it can be satisfied for the uniform distribution for instance.

For the boundaries $p_\th \in \{0,1\}$, note that whenever $p_\th = p_{\th '}$ for two types, incentive compatibility immediately implies that the wage contracts coincide $w_\th = w_{\th'}$.

\hfill \#


The following two examples consider a risk averse agent with strictly concave $u$. Although we cannot use the tractable $\th$-pointwise maximization of the principal's payoff when $u$ is non-linear, the examples illustrate how our characterization result can be used to derive qualitative features of optimal mechanisms. 
The formal proves of the propositions are relegated to Appendix \ref{sec:proofsApplications}. 

\paragraph{Example 3.}
Suppose $u(\cdot)$ is strictly concave. 
Let $C(\th, \mu) = (1-\th) K\Paren{\int_X z(x)\mu(\de x)}$, where $K$ is strictly increasing and convex and $z(\cdot)$ is differentiable, strictly increasing and convex.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:DegenerateExample}
    In any optimal mechanism for the above specification, for all $\th\colon$ $\mu_{\th} = \d_{\{x_\th\}}$ for some $x_\th \in X$.  
\end{proposition}
%The proof of this proposition is relegated to the Appendix.
This generalizes the observation in Example 1 that degenerate distributions are optimal when $z$ is convex. The risk aversion of the agent favors deterministic outcomes even further. As observed in Example 1, when the principal recommends a degenerate distribution, any deviation from this recommendation becomes observable. 
In these cases, the principal optimally reduced the combined hidden type and hidden action problem  to a pure hidden type problem; the hidden action friction disappears endogenously. 
\hfill \#



\paragraph{Example 4.}
Let $C(\th, \mu) = (1-\th) K\Paren{\int_X z(x)\mu(\de x)}$, where $K$ is strictly increasing and convex and $z(\cdot)$ is differentiable, strictly increasing and concave with  $z(\underline{x})=0$.
Now suppose that $u(\cdot)$ is strictly concave and for all $(A,B)\in\mathbb{R}^{2}_{++}$
\begin{align}\label{condition}-\frac{z^{\prime\prime}(x)}{z^{\prime}(x)}\geq -\frac{u^{\prime\prime}(A z(x)+B)}{u^{\prime}(A z(x)+B)}\times\left[\frac{Az^{\prime}(x)}{u^{\prime}(A z(x)+B)}\right]^{2} \quad \forall \ x \in [\underline{x},\overline{x}].
\end{align}
%\textit{Interpretation:} the cost function is more ``concave'' than the utility function. 


\begin{proposition}\label{prop:BinaryExample} 
 In any optimal mechanism for the above specification, for all $\th\colon$  $supp(\mu_{\theta}) \subseteq \{\underline{x},\overline{x}\}$.
\end{proposition}


By the Proposition, all distributions recommended have binary support at the extreme output realizations. Each distribution can again be identified by the probability of high output. Even when the principal's problem cannot conveniently be maximized $\th$-pointwise, the reduction to binary distribution allows us to write the principal's problem as a one-dimensional control problem with control $p_{\theta} \in [0,1]$. \hfill \#


