 %    %For various basic graph problems, several algorithms exist that computes the second optimal solution for the problem, such as, minimum spanning trees, shortest paths. 
 %    It has also been observed for various basic graph problems, such as minimum spanning trees (\cite{kMST/camerini1974k},\cite{kMSTibaraki}, \cite{kbst/mayr1992spanning}), 
 %    shortest paths (\cite{kshortestpath/bock1957algorithm}, \cite{kshortestpath/yen1971finding}, \cite{kShortestpath/ED1998}, \cite{kshortestpath/liamrodity}), that an equally important problem is to design an algorithm that computes the solution of the given problem and achieves the second optimal value.
 %    Moreover, in terms of comparing the level of fundamentality, it is noteworthy that these problems appear in most common books for the study of algorithms \cite{cormen2022introduction, DBLP:books/daglib/0069809}. %one of the most common books for the study of basic algorithms, namely, Introduction to Algorithms by Corman et al.    \cite{cormen2022introduction}

    The problem of designing efficient algorithms for computing $(s,t)$-mincut (or equivalently, maximum $(s,t)$-flow) has been studied for more than six decades. This problem is now almost settled with the recent breakthrough result on almost linear time algorithm for maximum $(s,t)$-flow by Van et al. \cite{van2023deterministic}. Interestingly, given an algorithm for maximum $(s,t)$-flow, we can compute all $(s,t)$-mincuts implicitly with an additional ${\mathcal O}(m)$ time, as shown by Picard and Queyranne \cite{DBLP:journals/mp/PicardQ80}. It is, therefore, natural to redefine the second $(s,t)$-mincut problem as follows. Design an algorithm that computes an $(s,t)$-cut of second minimum capacity. For brevity, henceforth we call $(s,t)$-cut of second minimum capacity by second $(s,t)$-mincut. 

    Vazirani and Yannakakis \cite{vazirani1992suboptimal} addressed the problem of computing an $(s,t)$-cut having $k^{th}$ minimum capacity in 1992. For computing any $k^{th}$ minimum capacity $(s,t)$-cut, they gave an algorithm that uses ${\mathcal O}(n^{2(k-1)})$ maximum $(s,t)$-flow computations. Conversely, they also argued, using NP-hardness of computing a maximum cut, that exponential dependence on $k$ is unavoidable assuming $P\ne NP$. %for computing any $(s,t)$-cut of $k^{th}$ minimum capacity. 
   To arrive at their result, the key problem they addressed is the design of an algorithm that computes a second $(s,t)$-mincut using ${\mathcal O}(n^2)$ maximum $(s,t)$-flow computations.
   % {\color{blue}They further improve the running time by designing a faster algorithm that computes a second $(s,t)$-mincut using only ${\mathcal O}(n)$ maximum $(s,t)$-flow computations. This algorithm has remained the state-of-the-art for more than three decades. Unfortunately, we show a serious error in the analysis of this faster algorithm (refer to Section \ref{sec : limitation}). As a result, the existing best-known algorithm for computing a second $(s,t)$-mincut uses ${\mathcal O}(n^2)$ maximum $(s,t)$-flow computations \cite{vazirani1992suboptimal}.}
   They further improve the running time by designing a faster algorithm that computes a second $(s,t)$-mincut using only ${\mathcal O}(n)$ maximum $(s,t)$-flow computations. 
   %This algorithm has remained the state-of-the-art for more than three decades. 
   We show that this faster algorithm is incorrect due to a serious error in its analysis (refer to Section \ref{sec : limitation} for details). As a result, the existing best-known algorithm for computing a second $(s,t)$-mincut uses ${\mathcal O}(n^2)$ maximum $(s,t)$-flow computations \cite{vazirani1992suboptimal}. 
    % To arrive at their result, the key problem they addressed is the design of an algorithm that computes a second $(s,t)$-mincut using ${\mathcal O}(n^2)$ maximum $(s,t)$-flow computations. 
    % {\color{red}Unfortunately, after more than three decades, this is the only known algorithm till date for computing a second $(s,t)$-mincut.}
    %As our first result, 
    We design an algorithm for the second $(s,t)$-mincut with a significantly improved running time as shown in the following theorem.
    % Vazirani and Yannakakis addressed the problem of designing an $(s,t)$-cut having $k^{th}$ minimum capacity in 1992. To arrive at their result, the key problem they addressed is the design of an efficient algorithm that computes a second $(s,t)$-mincut. They gave an algorithm that uses ${\mathcal O}(n^2)$ maximum $(s,t)$-flow computations for computing a second $(s,t)$-mincut.
    % Unfortunately, after more than three decades, this is the only known algorithm till date for computing a second $(s,t)$-mincut. In this paper, as our first result, we design the following algorithm that significantly improves the running time of this existing algorithm. for computing a second $(s,t)$-mincut.
    \begin{theorem} [Second $(s,t)$-mincut algorithm] \label{thm : second minimum (s,t)-cut}
        For any directed graph $G$ on $n$ vertices with integer edge capacities, %with a designated source vertex $s$ and a designated sink vertex $t$, 
        there is an algorithm that computes a second $(s,t)$-mincut in $G$ using $\Tilde{{\mathcal O}}(\sqrt{n})$ maximum $(s,t)$-flow computations with high probability.
    \end{theorem}
    % Karger \cite{karger1993} established that there are only ${\mathcal O}(n^2)$ global mincuts in a graph, where as, the number of $(s,t)$-mincuts can be exponential. So, it seems that any problem related to   
    The two well-known minimum cuts in a graph are $(s,t)$-mincut and global mincut. Recently, there has been a growing interest in understanding the difference in the complexity of computing an $(s,t)$-mincut and computing a global mincut. 
    %Recently, there has been a study of analyzing the time difference between computing $(s,t)$-mincut and global mincut. 
    For undirected weighted graphs, Li and Panigrahi \cite{DBLP:conf/focs/LiP20} established that the running time of computing a global mincut differs by only poly-logarithmic factors from the running time of computing an $(s,t)$-mincut. In particular, the authors showed that there is an algorithm that can compute a global mincut 
    %\arnote{in the time it takes for $\tilde{{\mathcal O}}(1)$ maximum $(s,t)$-flow computations}
    using $\tilde{{\mathcal O}}(1)$ maximum $(s,t)$-flow computations
   with additional $\Tilde{\mathcal{O}}(m)$ time. However, for directed weighted graphs, there is a \textit{large} gap between the running time of computing an $(s,t)$-mincut and computing a global mincut as follows. Cen et al. \cite{DBLP:conf/focs/Cen0NPSQ21} designed an algorithm that can compute a global mincut using $\tilde{\mathcal O}(\sqrt{n})$ maximum $(s,t)$-flow computations with additional $\tilde{\mathcal O}(m\sqrt{n})$ time. Interestingly, to arrive at our result in Theorem \ref{thm : second minimum (s,t)-cut}, 
    %we pursue the opposite direction and show that the second $(s,t)$-mincut can be computed using efficient algorithms for global mincut. In fact, 
    we show that the complexity of computing a global mincut is the same as the complexity of computing a second $(s,t)$-mincut \textit{modulo} a single maximum $(s,t)$-flow computation as follows, which might find many other important applications.


    % {\color{red}Recently, new algorithms have been designed for computing global mincut that use efficient algorithm for $(s,t)$-mincut \cite{DBLP:conf/focs/LiP20, DBLP:conf/focs/Cen0NPSQ21}. To arrive at our result in Theorem \ref{thm : second minimum (s,t)-cut}, we pursue the opposite direction and show that the second $(s,t)$-mincut can be computed using efficient algorithms for global mincut. In fact, we show that the complexity of computing a global mincut is the same as the complexity of computing a second $(s,t)$-mincut \textit{modulo} a single maximum $(s,t)$-flow computation as follows, which might find many other important applications.}
    %I and show that the second $(s,t)$-mincut can be computed using efficient algorithms for global mincut. In fact, we establish an equivalence between the two problems as follows.
    \begin{theorem} [Equivalence between global mincut and second $(s,t)$-mincut] \label{thm : equivalence between second st mincut and global}
        For any directed weighted graph $G$ on $n$ vertices and $m$ edges, the following two assertions hold.
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item The problem of computing a second $(s,t)$-mincut is reducible to the problem of computing a global mincut in ${\mathcal O}(MF(m,n))$ time, where $MF(m,n)$ denotes the time complexity for computing a maximum $(s,t)$-flow in $G$.
            \item The problem of computing a global mincut is reducible to the problem of computing a second $(s,t)$-mincut in $\mathcal{O}(m)$ time. 
        \end{enumerate}
    \end{theorem}
\begin{remark}
