\subsection{Dual Edge Sensitivity Oracle: Breaking the Quadratic Barrier}
%{\color{red} why do we refer to Minimum+1 (s,t) cut here ...} Minimum+1 $(s,t)$-cuts, as shown by Baswana, Bhanja and Pandey \cite{baswana2023minimum+}, play an important role in solving the problem of dual edge sensitivity oracle for $(s,t)$-mincut defined as follows.  
%Minimum+1 $(s,t)$-cuts, as shown by Baswana, Bhanja and Pandey \cite{baswana2023minimum+}, has an important application in the following 
The study of minimum+1 $(s,t)$-cuts often turns out to be useful in designing elegant \textit{dual edge sensitivity oracles} \cite{baswana2023minimum+, DBLP:conf/stoc/DinitzN95, DBLP:conf/icalp/Bhanja25}, which is defined as follows. 
% {\color{brown}once we say design oracle and once we say the problem is to design}
