It immediately follows from \Cref{prop : q2} that for any $(\lambda+1)$ $(s,t)$-cut $C$ in $G$, exactly one anchor edge contributes to $C$. This result leads to \Cref{prop : anchor edges property}(1) and provides a solution to \Cref{prob: lambda+1 strcuture} as follows.
\begin{property} \label{prop : q4}
    An $(s,t)$-cut $C$ is a $(\lambda+1)$ $(s,t)$-cut in $G$ if and only if $C$ satisfies property $\mathcal{P}$ in $\mathcal{S}(G\setminus \mathcal{A})$ and exactly one edge from $\mathcal{A}$ contributes to $C$.
\end{property}
By Property \ref{prop : q4}, our compact structure consists of $\mathcal{S}(G \setminus \mathcal{A})$ and $\mathcal{A}$. Using the best-known structure $\mathcal{D}_{PQ}(G)$ for storing and characterizing all $(s,t)$-mincuts \cite{DBLP:journals/mp/PicardQ80}, $\mathcal{D}_{PQ}(G \setminus \mathcal{A})$ occupies $\mathcal{O}(\min\{m,n\sqrt{\lambda}\})$ space. However, the cardinality of set $\mathcal{A}$ seems quadratic, since there can be $\Omega(n^2)$ edges carrying no flow in a maximum $(s,t)$-flow. So, we are only left to establish \Cref{prop : anchor edges property}(2).

To achieve a bound on $|{\mathcal A}|$, we show that there is a forest of $G$ that contains every anchor edge in $\mathcal{A}$. This result is obtained using the following structural insight on anchor edges. %based on \Cref{prop : q2}.
\begin{center}
    \textit{For any given maximum $(s,t)$-flow $f$, any cycle formed on the edges carrying zero flow in $f$, of graph $G$, cannot contain any anchor edge. (refer to Lemma \ref{lem : F has n-2 edges} in full version)}    
\end{center}
It immediately follows that $|{\mathcal A}|\le n-1$. 
By using Maxflow-Mincut Theorem, we further show that $\mathcal{A}$ can never contain more than $n-2$ edges, and hence, establishes \Cref{prop : anchor edges property}(2). This bound is trivially shown to be tight as well, and leads to Theorem \ref{thm: structure for min+1} finally.
}


