To characterize all $(\lambda+1)$ $(s,t)$-cuts  using $\mathcal{S}$, we aim to transform each $(\lambda+1)$ $(s,t)$-cut into an $(s,t)$-mincut by removing edges from $G$.
Observe that removal of a single edge from a $(\lambda+1)$ $(s,t)$-cut $C$ makes it an $(s,t)$-cut of capacity $\lambda$. 
However, it can reduce the capacity of $(s,t)$-mincut if the removed edge contributes to an $(s,t)$-mincut. Even if we can ensure the removed edges do not contribute to any $(s,t)$-mincut, the capacity of $(s,t)$-mincut may still be reduced. This is because at least a pair of removed edges can contribute to the same $(\lambda+1)$ $(s,t)$-cut.
Interestingly, we show that there exists a \textit{small} set of edges, denoted by $\mathcal{A}$, such that after the removal of all edges belonging to $\mathcal{A}$ from $G$, the following property holds in the resulting graph (refer to Lemma \ref{lem : min+1 as 1-transversal in G minus F} in full version). %\\

