\section{Randomized Algorithm: Finite-dimensional Euclidean Space}\label{sec:experts}

Considering the case of a finite predictor space $\mathcal{H}$, the learner has access to $N$ reference predictors, commonly referred to as experts in the context of online learning, at every time step. Each expert provides a prediction $h_i(\mathbf{x})$ for a given input vector $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^k$. We define a discrete measure $\delta$ over $\mathcal{H} = \{h_1, h_2 , \cdots , h_N\}$, such that each expert $h_i$ is assigned a probability $g_i$. The probability distribution over $\mathcal{H}$ is represented by the vector:
$$
\Delta^N =
\left\{ 
    g = (g_1, \cdots, g_N): 0 \leq g_i \leq 1, \text{for } i=1, \cdots, N \text{ and} \sum_i^N g_i = 1
\right\}.
$$
Here, $g_i = P \left(h_i \left(\mathbf{x}\right)\right)$  indicates the weight assigned to expert $i$'s prediction, and the set of all such vectors $g$ forms the standard $N$-simplex, denoted by $\Delta^{N}$.

At each time step $t$, after observing the pair $(\mathbf{x}_t, y_t)$, $\ell(y_t, h_i \left(\mathbf{x_t}\right))$ denotes the loss incurred when expert $i$ predicts $h_i$ based on $\mathbf{x_t}$. The expected loss under the current distribution $g_t$ is:
$$c_{(\mathbf{x}_t, y_t)}(g_t) = \sum_{i=1}^N \ell(y_t, h_i(\mathbf{x}_t)) g_t(h_i).$$
We initialize $g_1:=1$. To update the probability distribution for $t \geq 1$,
we first compute an intermediate vector using a gradient step:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{gradient_step}
    z_t(h_i) 
    &=& g_{t} (h_i) - \eta_{t} \nabla c_{(\mathbf{x}_t, y_t)}(g_t) \\
    &=& g_{t} (h_i) - \eta_{t} \ell(y_t, h_i(\mathbf{x}_t))\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
where $\eta_t$ is the learning rate. Since vector $z_t$ might not be a valid probability distribution as it may contain negative values or not sum to 1, we project it onto the simplex $\Delta_N$ using the proximal operator with an indicator function $\Psi$:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{prox_step}
    g_{t+1} 
    &=& \text{prox}_{\eta_{t} \Psi}(z_t) \\
    &=& \text{proj}_{\Delta^{N}}(z_t).\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
This projection ensures that the updated distribution $g_{t+1}$
remains a valid probability distribution over the set of experts $h_i$.


