asymmetry is proportional to the muon $C\!P$ asymmetry.  One can also notice how the new solutions extend to higher values of $\sigma$ for $\rho \sim \pi$. 
The same conclusions can be also drawn looking at the right panel of Fig.~7 where we show the constraints in the plane $\sigma$ versus $m_1$.

\subsubsection{$0\nu\b\b$ effective neutrino mass}

Finally, one can see from the right panel of Fig.~8 that flavour coupling does not really change the constraints in the plane $m_{ee}$ versus $m_1$.
The new solutions are confined within the region that was anyway accessible also without accounting for flavour coupling.


